  "slug": "bifurcation-topic-8fba",
  "summary": "Recent preprints connect spike-level mechanisms, multiscale V1 recovery, and connectome-constrained fly models to network-level dynamics.",
  "synthesis": "The shared thread is how structural and biophysical constraints in cortical and visual circuits shape network-level dynamics. A spatial leaky integrate-and-fire model maps spiking activity to activity and phase fields, linking turbulence-like dynamics to delayed propagation and dendritic filtering. In a multiscale macaque V1 model, deep network surrogates accelerate simulation roughly 10^5-fold, enabling stability analysis, and the new result is that similar visual tuning can conceal different recovery dynamics. A connectome-constrained fly visual model (FlyNet) is benchmarked against CNNs, RAFT and CTRNNs on collision detection, linking an adversarial and a perceptual illusion. Across these items, matched outputs do not guarantee matched dynamics; what differs is the trajectory or regime beneath them. The fly study is a benchmark rather than a dynamical-regime analysis, so this thread is dominant across the set rather than universal.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-16"
}
